Pick the LAN interface, not whatever the multicast route lands on.

mdnsd chooses its one IPv4 interface with mdns_get_default_ipv4(), which
UDP-connects to 224.0.0.251 and takes the source address Windows picks. On a
machine with WSL, Hyper-V or Docker installed that lands on a host-only virtual
switch: multicast is then joined and sent on an adapter no phone can see, and
the published A record points at an unroutable address. The receiver appears
perfectly healthy - it is simply advertising to nobody.

Windows unicast metrics do not help here (WiFi 35 vs vEthernet 5000 and it
still picked vEthernet), so choose explicitly: an adapter that is up, supports
multicast and has a default gateway. A host-only virtual switch has no gateway,
which is what separates it from the real LAN. Falls back to the original
heuristic if nothing qualifies.

--- a/lib/mdnsd/mdnsd.c
+++ b/lib/mdnsd/mdnsd.c
@@
 static uint32_t mdns_get_default_ipv4(void)
 {
     int fd;
     uint32_t addr = 0;
     struct sockaddr_in remote;
     struct sockaddr_in local;
     socklen_t local_len = sizeof(local);

+#ifdef WIN32
+    addr = mdns_pick_routable_ipv4();
+    if (addr) {
+        return addr;
+    }
+#endif
+
     fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0);
     if (fd == -1) {
         return 0;
     }
